    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d350">

                <pb facs="0434" n="322" xml:id="pg322"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">861.00/6005: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in
                        France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Wallace</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1920-01-07T19:00:00-05:00"><hi rendition="#italic">January 7,
                                1920, 7 p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>41. For Rathbone from Davis. Treasury R–148.</p>
                <p>Reference your R–174, December 27,<anchor n="39" xml:id="d350-f1"/> and
                    supplementing my R–141,<anchor n="40" xml:id="d350-f2"/> paragraph three.</p>
                <p>General Hines advises under date January 3, that repairs on the two transports
                    allotted for this movement, namely, the <hi rendition="#italic">America</hi> and
                        <hi rendition="#italic">President Grant</hi>, will consume approximately
                    fifteen days. If resurvey confirms this, these vessels will be unable to arrive
                    Vladivostok before February 10, although every effort being made to expedite
                    arrival at Vladivostok by working day and night on repairs. As soon as vessels
                    sail from New York shall advise you definite date arrival first ship
                    Vladivostok. Norman H. Davis, Assistant Secretary of the Treasury.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Lansing</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
